Product Requirements Document (PRD): BugSig
Tagline: Autonomous QA Analyst for High-Velocity Engineering Teams.
Core Value: Eliminates the "Review Triage" tax by autonomously converting user noise into deduplicated, root-cause-analyzed engineering tickets.

1. Architecture & Tech Stack (Production Grade)
We move from "scripts" to "services."

Frontend (The Console): Next.js 14 (App Router), TanStack Query (Caching), Zustand (State), Shadcn UI (Design System).

Backend (The Orchestrator): FastAPI (Async Python), Pydantic (Strict Validation).

The Brain (AI Layer):

Memory: ChromaDB (Local Vector Store) for semantic deduplication.

Reasoning: DeepSeek-R1 (via API) for Root Cause Analysis (RCA).

Embeddings: all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (Local inference) for privacy/speed.

Data Layer:

SQLite (Relational State: Users, Projects, Tickets).

Redis (Optional: Job Queue for large CSV uploads).

Deployment: Docker Compose (Microservices architecture).

2. Development Phases (The Startup Execution Plan)
Phase 1: The "Intelligent Ingestion" Engine (Backend Alpha)
Objective: Build a system that "remembers" issues and prevents duplicates.
What to build:

Semantic Deduplication Service: A robust pipeline that embeds incoming reviews and queries ChromaDB. If cosine_similarity > 0.85, it merges data into the existing cluster; otherwise, it creates a new one.

Metadata Extractor Service: A Regex/NLP hybrid module that parses unstructured text to extract "Hard Signals" (Version v2.4, Device Pixel 7, Error Code 500).

The "Noise Filter": A classifier (Zero-shot or Heuristic) that instantly discards "useless" reviews (e.g., "Good app", "Thanks") so the expensive LLM only sees actual problems.

API Layer: POST /upload endpoint that accepts a CSV, runs the pipeline asynchronously, and returns a job_id.

Phase 2: The "Reasoning" Core (DeepSeek Integration)
Objective: Move from "clustering" to "engineering analysis."
What to build:

Context-Aware Prompting: Design a prompt chain for DeepSeek-R1 that takes a cluster of reviews + metadata and outputs a structured JSON object containing:

Hypothesis: Technical theory of failure.

Reproduction Path: Step-by-step user actions.

Severity Score: Calculated based on sentiment + volume.

Regression Detector: A logic module that compares current cluster volume against historical baselines. (e.g., "Login Error" spiked 300% in v2.5).

Ticket Generator: Formats the AI output into Markdown compatible with JIRA/GitHub Issues.

Phase 3: The "Mission Control" (Frontend MVP)
Objective: A professional SaaS interface for Product Managers/Dev Leads.
What to build:

The Triage Board (Kanban): A drag-and-drop board (New, Triaged, Ignored) populated by the AI.

Issue Detail View: A slide-over panel showing:

The "AI Diagnosis" (RCA).

The "Evidence Locker" (5 representative user reviews).

The "Version Heatmap" (Visual chart of affected versions).

Sync Actions: A "Push to JIRA" button (mocked for MVP, but architected to support webhooks).

Phase 4: The "Polish & Launch" (MLOps & Docs)
Objective: Make it runnable and demonstrable.
What to build:

Dockerization: A docker-compose.yml that spins up Frontend, Backend, and ChromaDB with one command.

Demo Data Seeding: A script that pre-loads the DB with a "Story" (e.g., "The v2.5 Crash Incident") so the dashboard looks alive immediately.

Documentation: A README.md that explains the Hybrid Architecture (Local Embeddings + Cloud Reasoning) and the Deduplication Logic.

3. The "Startup" Features (Differentiation)
"Regression Alerting": Competitors show you what is wrong. BugSig warns you when you broke it (Correlation with Version).

"Permanent Memory": Competitors re-analyze every upload from scratch. BugSig maintains a Vector History, so Issue #45 tracks specific bug behavior over weeks/months.

"Technical RCA": Competitors give summaries ("People are mad"). BugSig gives Hypotheses ("NullRef in Auth Module").

